"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[20165],{20165:e=>{e.exports="# Schema, Storage & Maintenance\n\nRedshift schema objects, table definitions, constraints, layout hints, and table-maintenance commands → DBSQL equivalents.\n\n> **Databricks defaults**: Managed tables are **Delta format** by default — no `USING DELTA` clause needed. Reference permanent objects using **Unity Catalog three-part names**: `catalog.schema.table`.\n\n## REDSHIFT\n\n### DISTSTYLE, DISTKEY, SORTKEY, ENCODE — strip all\n\nRedshift table-layout hints have **no DBSQL equivalent**. Delta stores tables in columnar format automatically, and liquid clustering (`CLUSTER BY AUTO`) replaces manual distribution tuning.\n\n| Redshift clause                           | Action                                                             |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `DISTSTYLE ALL` / `EVEN` / `KEY` / `AUTO` | Remove                                                             |\n| `DISTKEY(col)`                            | Remove. If a hot join column is known, consider `CLUSTER BY (col)` |\n| `SORTKEY (col1, col2)`                    | Remove. Consider `CLUSTER BY (col1, col2)` or `ZORDER BY`          |\n| `COMPOUND SORTKEY (...)`                  | Remove. Same options as `SORTKEY`                                  |\n| `INTERLEAVED SORTKEY (...)`               | Remove. Consider `ZORDER BY` (closest analogue for ad-hoc filters) |\n| `ENCODE <type>` on individual columns     | Remove — Delta encodes automatically                               |\n| `BACKUP NO` / `BACKUP YES`                | Remove                                                             |\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nCREATE TABLE sales (\n    id        BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1) NOT NULL,\n    region_id INT    ENCODE RAW,\n    amount    DECIMAL(18,2) ENCODE AZ64\n)\nDISTSTYLE KEY\nDISTKEY(region_id)\nCOMPOUND SORTKEY(region_id, id);\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.sales (\n    id        BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,\n    region_id INT,\n    amount    DECIMAL(18,2)\n)\nCLUSTER BY AUTO;\n```\n\n`CLUSTER BY AUTO` lets Delta pick clustering columns based on observed query patterns — prefer this unless you have a known hot column.\n\n### Identity Columns\n\n> **WARNING: Identity columns have significant implications in Databricks.**\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nCREATE TABLE t (\n    id    BIGINT IDENTITY(1,1),\n    name  VARCHAR(100)\n);\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t (\n    id    BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,\n    name  STRING\n);\n```\n\n**Critical limitations:**\n\n1. **Disables parallel writes** — Tables with identity columns cannot be written concurrently.\n2. **Values not guaranteed sequential** — May have gaps; not in insertion order.\n3. **Cannot be added later** — No `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN ... GENERATED AS IDENTITY`.\n4. **No `SCOPE_IDENTITY()` equivalent**.\n\n**Preferred alternative — UUID (allows parallel writes):**\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t (id STRING DEFAULT UUID(), name STRING);\n```\n\nIf the Redshift source used `IDENTITY(seed, step)` with a non-default seed, carry the start value:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE ... (id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY (START WITH 1000 INCREMENT BY 1));\n```\n\n### Indexes and Constraints\n\nRedshift has no indexes (columnar storage). Primary-key, foreign-key, and unique constraints exist but are **informational-only** (not enforced). Same behavior in DBSQL:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift: informational PK/FK\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n  id INT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,\n  customer_id INT REFERENCES customers(id)\n);\n\n-- DBSQL: informational (not enforced); use Unity Catalog three-part names\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders (\n  id INT NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY,\n  customer_id INT,\n  CONSTRAINT fk_customer FOREIGN KEY (customer_id)\n    REFERENCES my_catalog.my_schema.customers(id) NOT ENFORCED\n);\n```\n\nCHECK constraints **are enforced** in both Redshift and DBSQL — behavior matches.\n\n### ALTER TABLE — column changes\n\n| Redshift                                       | DBSQL                                          | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| ---------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN c INT`               | `ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMN c INT`               | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| `ALTER TABLE t DROP COLUMN c`                  | `ALTER TABLE t DROP COLUMN c`                  | **Requires Delta column mapping** — enable once per table with `ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.columnMapping.mode' = 'name')`. Dropped data is soft-deleted until `REORG TABLE t APPLY (PURGE)` rewrites the files |\n| `ALTER TABLE t RENAME COLUMN old TO new`       | `ALTER TABLE t RENAME COLUMN old TO new`       | Same column-mapping prerequisite as `DROP COLUMN` — enable once with the `TBLPROPERTIES` set above                                                                                                                          |\n| `ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN c TYPE BIGINT`     | Not supported                                  | Cannot change column type on a Delta table. Cast in queries or recreate.                                                                                                                                                    |\n| `ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN c SET DEFAULT ...` | `ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN c SET DEFAULT ...` | Same                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| `ALTER TABLE t ALTER DISTKEY`/`ALTER SORTKEY`  | Remove                                         | No clustering-style change DDL; Delta's liquid clustering adapts at runtime                                                                                                                                                 |\n\n### DEFAULT Constraints\n\nRedshift DEFAULT expressions map directly; function names differ for a few cases:\n\n| Redshift default           | DBSQL equivalent            |\n| -------------------------- | --------------------------- |\n| `DEFAULT GETDATE()`        | `DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` |\n| `DEFAULT SYSDATE`          | `DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` |\n| `DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE`     | `DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE`      |\n| `DEFAULT UUID()`           | `DEFAULT UUID()`            |\n| `DEFAULT 0` / `DEFAULT ''` | Same                        |\n\n### CREATE TABLE AS SELECT (CTAS)\n\nIdentical syntax, but strip DISTKEY/SORTKEY:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nCREATE TABLE staging.events_agg DISTKEY(event_date) SORTKEY(event_date) AS\nSELECT event_date, COUNT(*) FROM events GROUP BY event_date;\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.staging.events_agg AS\nSELECT event_date, COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM my_catalog.raw.events GROUP BY event_date;\n```\n\n### Views\n\n`CREATE [OR REPLACE] VIEW` works identically. Redshift `WITH NO SCHEMA BINDING` has no DBSQL equivalent — drop the clause; Delta views always re-resolve.\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nCREATE OR REPLACE VIEW v_active_orders AS\nSELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'active' WITH NO SCHEMA BINDING;\n\n-- DBSQL\nCREATE OR REPLACE VIEW my_catalog.my_schema.v_active_orders AS\nSELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.orders WHERE status = 'active';\n```\n\n### External tables (`CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE`)\n\nRedshift Spectrum external tables become **Unity Catalog external tables** over the same storage:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift Spectrum\nCREATE EXTERNAL TABLE spectrum.logs (\n  ts TIMESTAMP, msg VARCHAR(4000)\n)\nROW FORMAT SERDE 'org.apache.hadoop.hive.serde2.OpenCSVSerde'\nLOCATION 's3://bucket/logs/';\n\n-- DBSQL (UC external table)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.spectrum.logs (\n  ts TIMESTAMP, msg STRING\n) USING CSV LOCATION 's3://bucket/logs/';\n```\n\nConfigure the storage credential and external location in Unity Catalog separately — the inline `CREDENTIALS 'aws_iam_role=...'` clause has no DDL equivalent.\n\n### COMMENT ON\n\n`COMMENT ON` is supported in both dialects — only the table name needs three-part qualification:\n\n```sql\n-- Redshift\nCOMMENT ON TABLE orders IS 'order fact table';\n\n-- DBSQL\nCOMMENT ON TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders IS 'order fact table';\n-- or inline: CREATE TABLE ... COMMENT 'order fact table'\n```\n\n### Maintenance commands — VACUUM, ANALYZE\n\n| Redshift                                               | Action                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `VACUUM [FULL \\| SORT ONLY \\| DELETE ONLY \\| REINDEX]` | Remove — Redshift VACUUM (space reclamation + sort-key maintenance) has no direct DBSQL equivalent; Delta auto-compacts via `OPTIMIZE` / predictive optimization. **Don't substitute DBSQL `VACUUM`** — it cleans up time-travel files, a different concept that can break time-travel queries. |\n| `ANALYZE` / `ANALYZE <table>`                          | Remove when used as routine maintenance — DBSQL maintains stats automatically. **Translate to `ANALYZE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.t COMPUTE STATISTICS`** when the source explicitly runs it before a planner-sensitive query (e.g. immediately after a bulk load).                             |\n| `ANALYZE COMPRESSION <table>`                          | Remove — Delta encodes columns automatically.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n\n### Session-tuning commands — remove\n\nThese Redshift `SET` parameters have no DBSQL equivalent:\n\n| Redshift                                    | Action                                              |\n| ------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SET enable_result_cache_for_session = OFF` | Remove — DBSQL has its own result cache             |\n| `SET wlm_query_slot_count = N`              | Remove — use warehouse/cluster sizing               |\n| `SET statement_timeout`                     | Remove — configure at SQL warehouse level           |\n| `SET query_group`                           | Remove                                              |\n| `SET mv_enable_aqmv_for_session`            | Remove                                              |\n| `SET enable_case_sensitive_identifier`      | Remove — handle case-sensitivity in schema or query |\n| `SET search_path`                           | Replace with `USE SCHEMA catalog.schema`            |\n\n### Optimization in DBSQL\n\n```sql\n-- Frequently filtered columns → ZORDER on existing tables\nOPTIMIZE my_catalog.my_schema.orders ZORDER BY (customer_id, order_date);\n\n-- New tables → liquid clustering (preferred)\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders (...) CLUSTER BY AUTO;\n\n-- Explicit cluster keys when a hot column is known\nCREATE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.orders (...) CLUSTER BY (customer_id);\n```\n"}}]);
